WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.190
- All right, we've got
time for one last story.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.980
And it's all about hope and
the power of human connection.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:05.818
This is What I'm Liking.

00:00:05.818 --> 00:00:07.825
(upbeat music)

00:00:07.825 --> 00:00:08.658
(audience applauding)

00:00:08.658 --> 00:00:10.472
This month marked 21 years

00:00:10.472 --> 00:00:12.894
since the worst terrorist
attack on our nation.

00:00:12.894 --> 00:00:14.370
Like so many of us,

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.414
a third grade teacher in
Illinois felt helpless

00:00:16.414 --> 00:00:17.828
after the attacks on 9/11.

00:00:17.828 --> 00:00:20.040
She wanted to help her students

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.770
and kids in New York City heal.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.090
So she had her class write letters,

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:24.990
and she sent them

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.134
to a random third grade
classroom in New York.

00:00:27.134 --> 00:00:29.646
That led to a year long pen pal program,

00:00:29.646 --> 00:00:32.730
but there was no way back
then she could have known

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.400
it would also lead to a
decades long friendship

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.679
and now a new book.

00:00:36.679 --> 00:00:38.367
We have that teacher on the line right now

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:40.620
along with the teacher
from the New York class.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.653
Everybody say hello to Elaine and Julie.

00:00:42.653 --> 00:00:43.686
Hello.

00:00:43.686 --> 00:00:45.807
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:00:45.807 --> 00:00:46.640
- Hi, Kelly.
- Hi, Kelly.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:47.473
- Hi ladies.

00:00:47.473 --> 00:00:50.520
So first of all, thank you
so much for being teachers.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:51.870
You are angels amongst us.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:53.250
I love teachers so much.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.680
You are just selfless
people, and I appreciate you.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.960
So I just wanted to say that first.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.807
But also, tell us about those-
- Thank you so much.

00:00:59.807 --> 00:01:00.813
- Absolutely.

00:01:00.813 --> 00:01:02.730
Tell us about those first letters though

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:03.736
and what grew from there.

00:01:03.736 --> 00:01:05.423
- Well, after 9/11,

00:01:05.423 --> 00:01:09.048
most Americans wanted
to do something to help.

00:01:09.048 --> 00:01:14.048
And we adults could donate
money, we could do blood drives.

00:01:14.247 --> 00:01:17.130
But when you're eight and nine years old,

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.580
you really are limited
in what you could do.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.043
But I knew my students
could probably write letters

00:01:23.043 --> 00:01:24.691
that would show that they cared.

00:01:24.691 --> 00:01:27.960
And I took those letters to
the Yorkville Post Office.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.466
And the clerk there

00:01:29.466 --> 00:01:33.045
picked out a random
zip code for Manhattan,

00:01:33.045 --> 00:01:35.418
put it on the envelope,

00:01:35.418 --> 00:01:38.730
and it was addressed to
any third grade class.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:39.810
And we had no clue

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.510
whether they would be
delivered or received.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.920
- Wow. And Julie, how
did you end up with them?

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.430
- Well, out of all the
third grade teachers

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.540
in all of New York City,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.538
the letters ended up in my mailbox,

00:01:50.538 --> 00:01:52.552
which was amazing chance.

00:01:52.552 --> 00:01:56.100
And it was really one
of the sweetest things

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.735
that anyone could have done at the time.

00:01:57.735 --> 00:02:00.009
And after we excitedly wrote back,

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:03.660
we started a year long relationship

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:08.660
where the classes exchanged
drawings and holiday cards.

00:02:08.711 --> 00:02:12.780
And also, Elaine got to visit my class.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.940
And I got to visit Elaine's class.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.772
And we started what became
a 21 year friendship.

00:02:17.772 --> 00:02:20.010
- That is so cool, y'all.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:20.843
And now-

00:02:20.843 --> 00:02:21.676
(audience applauding)

00:02:21.676 --> 00:02:22.509
Now you've written

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:24.600
a book called the United
States of Friendship.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.493
So why did you wanna write that?

00:02:26.493 --> 00:02:30.917
- Well, there's been a whole
generation born since 9/11.

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:32.850
And they don't have a clue

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.027
about all of the good things that happened

00:02:35.027 --> 00:02:37.672
out of something so bad.

00:02:37.672 --> 00:02:39.360
And so I wrote to Julie

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.070
and asked her if she was willing

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:45.433
to tell our story again in a book form.

00:02:45.433 --> 00:02:47.190
And she agreed.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:50.316
And so we wrote it as a conversation

00:02:50.316 --> 00:02:54.314
about the actual events that
happened to us at that time.

00:02:54.314 --> 00:02:56.970
And I wrote my memories in red ink,

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.083
and she wrote hers in blue.

00:02:59.083 --> 00:03:02.400
We finished our book at
the end with the phrase

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.145
our unity is our strength.

00:03:04.145 --> 00:03:07.800
And when we come together
as a united country,

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.715
that's when we really are the strongest.

00:03:09.715 --> 00:03:11.010
- Oh, I love this.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:11.993
Thank you so much, Elaine.

00:03:11.993 --> 00:03:13.262
Thank you, Julie.

00:03:13.262 --> 00:03:14.095
(crowd applauding)
(crowd cheering)

00:03:14.095 --> 00:03:15.180
This is amazing-
- Thank you.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.470
- sharing this with all of us.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.033
I'm liking your post right now, alright?

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:19.510
(bell dinging)
- [Elaine] Yay.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:20.429
- And that's not all.

00:03:20.429 --> 00:03:23.670
Our season-long partner
Pilot pen makers of G2,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.740
the go-to pen of The Kelly Clarkson Show,

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.500
want to honor truly unstoppable
people just like y'all.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.140
So they're gonna be writing
you a check for $1000.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.004
That's $500 each.

00:03:32.004 --> 00:03:32.837
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:03:32.837 --> 00:03:33.938
(woman laughing)
- Oh, wow.

00:03:33.938 --> 00:03:34.771
- Oh my gosh.

00:03:34.771 --> 00:03:36.886
Thank you. That's amazing.
- Thank you.

00:03:36.886 --> 00:03:38.250
- Thank you ladies.
(crowd applauding)

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.162
Thank you so much for being educators.

00:03:40.162 --> 00:03:41.910
Thank you for joining us,

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.350
and thank you to all our guests today,

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:44.410
Billy Eichner, Luke McFarland-

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:45.243
(audience applauding)

00:03:45.243 --> 00:03:47.160
Jim Rash, Simone Press, and Carley.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:47.993
Y'all have a great day.

00:03:47.993 --> 00:03:49.357
If its not, try and change it.

00:03:49.357 --> 00:03:50.190
(audience applauding)

